# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Data IS committed, deliberately.
#
# The usual "never commit data" rule exists because data churns: git stores a
# full snapshot of every version, so a file that changes weekly balloons the
# repo. Ours is frozen by team ground rule and will never change, so there is
# exactly one version of it forever. The rule does not apply.
#
# Committing it is what actually enforces "one frozen dataset, nobody
# re-downloads mid-project". The upstream Kaggle dataset is refreshed after
# every Grand Prix, so a teammate who downloads next week gets DIFFERENT data
# and incomparable results. Pinning it in git makes that impossible.
#
# 29 MB total, largest file 24 MB (lap_times.csv). GitHub warns at 50 MB and
# blocks at 100 MB per file, so we are well inside limits.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# features.csv is a derived convenience copy for opening in Excel. It must
# never be the source of truth - CSV loses dtypes (int8 -> int64) and cannot
# distinguish NaN from an empty string, which would silently break the
# missing-data experiment.
data/processed/features.csv
